>A myth (but one of some centuries' standing). It derives from the
>Old English genitive -es case ending.


There are a couple languages which handle possessives that way, among
them Finnish, Hungarian, and (I think) Hebrew. e.g. in Hungarian: 'az
alma' = 'the apple'; 'az almaja' = 'his apple'; 'az almaja Karoly' =
'Charles's apple (translated word-for-word, 'his apple Charles').

However, this is an atypical construction not generally found in
Indo-European languages.

